From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: thelamer <ryankuba@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 11:14:51 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Code the macroblocks a caller names copies of the previous input

A page scroll moves most of a frame whole, and x264 found each moved macroblock again by a search:
UMH over 64 samples at full resolution, which finds a 24-sample scroll and codes a 120-sample one
as new picture. The caller can now name a macroblock a copy of the previous input picture at a
whole-sample vector (X264_MBINFO_COPY in mb_info, the vector in mb_copy, one a macroblock). x264
takes it as a candidate: where it still holds the previous input (a picture passed in place, until
the next x264_encoder_encode returns or x264_encoder_release_input) it compares the macroblock with
that picture at the vector (x264_mb_copy_check: the luma always, the chroma at an even vector,
since an odd one moves the chroma sites), and codes a macroblock that matches as the reference at
that vector without a search; one that differs, or whose vector reaches past the picture or the
level's range, or of a picture x264 no longer holds, is coded as without the flag. A macroblock
matched whole whose P_SKIP vector is the hint is a P_SKIP, on the run path of the
constant-macroblock skip (mb_predict_mv_pskip_frame reads the prediction from the frame's arrays);
one matched in luma alone is a P_SKIP where x264's own probe finds the residual quantizes away,
else a 16x16 block at the vector. The reference's effective QP is read over the macroblocks the
copy reads, as the constant skip reads it for one. The lookahead keeps its own search, so the
rate control plans the frame as it did without hints: scoring a hinted block at its vector made a
CRF session code a scroll at QP 21.5 in place of 32.3, half again the bytes.

Measured with four sliced threads on four pinned cores of an idle Ryzen 9 5950X at 2560x1600, NV12
in place, the vectors from the content's known motion, against the same frames without them. At
one quantizer (25) a 24-sample scroll took 0.95 ms of a frame where it took 1.38, at the same
bytes; a 120-sample scroll 2.42 ms and 29.2 kB where it took 2.76 and 57.3; a window dragged by an
odd vector 0.90 where 0.85. At CRF 25 the 24-sample scroll took 2.32 ms where it took 2.59 at the
same QP and bytes, and the 120-sample one coded in 25.8 kB a frame where it took 42.1. Without
the flag every stream comes out bit for bit as before (the 126 runs of the previous patch), and
with it the run path codes the stream the full path codes (18 runs: CRF at one and four threads
and VBV at one, the two scrolls and the drag, at CRF 25 and at QP 25).
